SLUG: services/packing-unpacking.html
TITLE: Packing & Unpacking Services in Kanpur
DESC: Professional packing in Kanpur with multi-layer wrapping, room-labelled cartons and bagged furniture hardware — plus unpacking and removal of packing waste.
HEADING: Packing &amp; unpacking
LEAD: The part of a move that decides whether anything breaks. Done properly it is unremarkable, which is the point.
CRUMBS: Home|/ ; Services|/services.html ; Packing &amp; unpacking|
---

  <section class="section on-paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split is-wide-left">
        <div>
          <h2>Why things break</h2>
          <p class="lead">
            Almost never from a dropped carton. Breakages come from movement
            inside the box — items touching each other, or sliding into empty
            space, for hours.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is why a well-packed carton is a full carton. Fragile items
            are wrapped individually so nothing touches, then packed with fill
            so nothing shifts, then boxed to a weight a person can carry
            properly. A half-empty carton is more dangerous than a heavy one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every carton is labelled with its room and its contents before it
            leaves the floor. It costs a few seconds and it is the difference
            between unpacking and excavating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <span class="card__icon"><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-box"/></svg></span>
          <h3>Materials, matched to the item</h3>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><strong>Bubble wrap</strong> for glass, crockery and screens</span></li>
            <li><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><strong>Corrugated sheets</strong> around furniture edges and table tops</span></li>
            <li><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><strong>Stretch film</strong> over upholstery and drawers</span></li>
            <li><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><strong>Double-wall cartons</strong> for books and anything dense</span></li>
            <li><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><strong>Wardrobe cartons</strong> so hanging clothes travel on the rail</span></li>
            <li><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><strong>Labelled bags</strong> for furniture hardware, taped to the item</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section on-sunk">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Saving money</span>
        <h2>How to bring the packing cost down</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Packing is usually the largest single line on a full-service quote,
          and it is also the easiest one to reduce without taking on risk.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Pack the safe things yourself</h3>
          <p>
            Clothes, books, linen and shoes are hard to damage and slow to
            pack. Doing these yourself removes a large share of the labour and
            material while leaving the fragile work to us.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Declutter before the survey</h3>
          <p>
            You pay to move volume. Anything you are not going to unpack at the
            other end is worth dealing with before we count it, not after.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Skip unpacking if you are not in a hurry</h3>
          <p>
            Many people want the kitchen and beds unpacked on day one and are
            happy to work through the rest themselves. We can do a partial
            unpack and collect the empty cartons later.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section on-paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="wrap-narrow">
        <h2>Questions we get asked</h2>
        <div class="faq mt-6" data-faq>
          <details>
            <summary>Can you pack only the kitchen and breakables?</summary>
            <div class="faq__body"><p>
              Yes, and it is the most common arrangement we scope. Tell us
              which rooms you want to handle yourself and the quote is built
              around that split.
            </p></div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>If I pack my own boxes, are they covered?</summary>
            <div class="faq__body"><p>
              Owner-packed cartons are normally excluded from transit cover,
              because nobody can verify what went in or how. That is true of
              movers generally, not just us — so pack the hard-to-damage things
              yourself and leave the fragile items to be professionally packed.
            </p></div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do you take the empty cartons away?</summary>
            <div class="faq__body"><p>
              Yes, if you want. We can unpack and remove the waste on the same
              visit, or come back once you have unpacked at your own pace.
            </p></div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How many days before the move do you pack?</summary>
            <div class="faq__body"><p>
              For most homes, the day before, or the same morning for smaller
              flats. Larger houses with full packing may need two days. We
              agree the schedule at survey so you know which rooms become
              unusable and when.
            </p></div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


